{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need count of satisfying assignments for CNF with 12 variables. Clauses list: \n1: [-4,12] meaning ¬x4 ∨ x12\n2: [-6,7] ¬x6 ∨ x7\n3: [-10,1] ¬x10 ∨ x1\n4: [-8,7] ¬x8 ∨ x7\n5: [-3,11] ¬x3 ∨ x11\n6: [-2,12] ¬x2 ∨ x12\n7: [4,2] x4 ∨ x2\n8: [-5,1] ¬x5 ∨ x1\n9: [5,10] x5 ∨ x10\n10: [6,8] x6 ∨ x8\n11: [3,9] x3 ∨ x9\n12: [-9,11] ¬x9 ∨ x11\n\nWe need count of assignments satisfying all. Let's analyze constraints.\n\nWe can treat as implications. Convert each clause to implications: (¬a ∨ b) is a → b. For clause [a,b] (both positive) is (¬a ∨ b) ∧ (¬b ∨ a). So we have equivalence between a and b.\n\nLet's list:\n\nClause1: ¬x4 ∨ x12 => x4 → x12\nClause2: ¬x6 ∨ x7 => x6 → x7\nClause3: ¬x10 ∨ x1 => x10 → x1\nClause4: ¬x8 ∨ x7 => x8 → x7\nClause5: ¬x3 ∨ x11 => x3 → x11\nClause6: ¬x2 ∨ x12 => x2 → x12\nClause7: [4,2] gives x4 → x2 and x2 → x4 (equivalence)\nClause8: ¬x5 ∨ x1 => x5 → x1\nClause9: [5,10] gives x5 → x10 and x10 → x5 (equivalence)\nClause10: [6,8] gives x6 → x8 and x8 → x6 (equivalence)\nClause11: [3,9] gives x3 → x9 and x9 → x3 (equivalence)\nClause12: ¬x9 ∨ x11 => x9 → x11\n\nNow we can deduce equivalence classes:\n\nFrom clause7: x4 ≡ x2\nFrom clause9: x5 ≡ x10\nFrom clause10: x6 ≡ x8\nFrom clause11: x3 ≡ x9\n\nAlso we have implications:\n\nx4 → x12\nx2 → x12 (but x2 ≡ x4 so same)\nx6 → x7\nx8 → x7 (but x8 ≡ x6 so same)\nx10 → x1\nx5 → x1 (but x5 ≡ x10 so same)\nx3 → x11\nx9 → x11 (but x9 ≡ x3 so same)\n\nAlso x9 → x11 already.\n\nAlso we have x6→x7, x8→x7 same.\n\nNow we have no implications from x7 to others, etc.\n\nThus the formula constraints are:\n\nEquivalence groups: \nG1: {x4, x2}\nG2: {x5, x10}\nG3: {x6, x8}\nG4: {x3, x9}\n\nOther variables: x1, x7, x11"}}],"created":1790961335,"model":"C:\\Users\\d2j3\\Documents\\Codex\\2026-09-10\\napraw\\outputs\\moon-native\\models\\gpt-oss-20b-MXFP4.gguf","system_fingerprint":"b10809-5266f24da","object